package pages

templ SyntaxImportsPage() {
	<h1>Imports</h1>
	<ul>
		<li>
			There are two kinds of imports in ghoti
			<ul>
				<li>Library imports: These are imports denoted by identifiers and are declared in dedicated 'library' files</li>
				<li>File imports: These are imports denoted by a string that is a file path, followed by an identifier alias</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>The standard file extension for ghoti files is <code>.gh</code></li>
		<li>Both file and library imports allow an identifier alias using the <code>as</code> keyword, with this being required for user imports</li>
		<li>An import can be marked public in order to expose it to the outside world when the enclosing file is imported</li>
		<li>Note that these statements are private by default and can be prefixed with <code>pub</code> to unrestrict visibility</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `import std; // Library import
import std as stud; // Library import with alias
import "node.gh"; // Illegal, file imports always require alias
pub import "node.gh" as node; // Public file import with alias` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<ul>
		<li>The filepath given to the import should be relative to the asking file, <em>not</em> to a local project 'root'</li>
		<li>Import statements can be used anywhere in your program, though they do not yield a value (hence why they are not called expressions)</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>Library Import Declaration</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Libraries are declared by indicating that a file is a root of an import tree</li>
		<li>A library's root file path must be provided to the compiler through the command line</li>
		<li>These imports can be aliased to avoid name collisions, though this is not required</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>File Import Declaration</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>A file in ghoti is importable through its path (relative or absolute) to the importing file</li>
		<li>These imports must be aliased to an identifier as the string path cannot be used implicitly as a namespace</li>
	</ul>
	<h3>Example</h3>
	<ul>
		<li><code>std.gh</code> declares</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `import "types/option.gh" as option;
pub import "io/io.gh" as io;
pub const Option = option.Option;
...` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<ul>
		<li><code>io/io.gh</code> declares a public function <code>println</code> which can be accessed through the member access operator (<code>.</code>):</li>
	</ul>
	<pre><code class="language-ghoti">{ `_ = std.io.println(...);` }</code></pre>
	<ul>
		<li><code>types/option.gh</code> declares a public function <code>Option</code> that returns a generically-typed union</li>
	</ul>
	<pre><code class="language-ghoti">{ `pub const Option = fn(comptime T: type): type {...};` }</code></pre>
	<ul>
		<li>This can be used just like any other function through the same <code>.</code> operator; there is no separate module access operator</li>
		<li><code>option</code> is a private import of <code>std.gh</code>, so the type constructor is only reachable through its public alias:</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `let mut a: std.Option(i32) = .{ .none = {} };     // Allowed due to the public alias
let mut b: std.option.Option(i32) = .{ .none = {} }; // Illegal, 'option' is not a public import` }
		</code>
	</pre>
}
